#{[17592186519597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146410" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519597 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{1,2,3\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,4\\}.\n$$\n\nFind an expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<1,y<0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eeeede00-ac86-5992-a122-f787740a8bf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519597 #edb/ref 1074 "If $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\sum }_{{x}_{i}\\le x}{\\sum }_{{y}_{i}\\le y}f({x}_{i},{y}_{i}).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- We can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{1,2,3\\}\\times \\{1,2,4\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's sketch the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<1$ and $y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/8e/8edc6bb45750996ba8d84bf946eaf4de6c68c0f3074ab7af4bee2b68c48c2095.png)\n\nSince every value in the support of $X$ is at least $1$ and $x<1,$ no possible value of $X$ satisfies $X\\le x.$ Also, every value in the support of $Y$ is at least $1$ and $y<0,$ so no possible value of $Y$ satisfies $Y\\le y.$\n\nTherefore, there are no points in the joint support that lie in the region $Δ,$ and\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1069 "q-146847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(12,4)=0.6,\\,F(9,4)=0.3,\\,F(3,4)=0.15,\n$$\n\nand the supports of $X$ and $Y$, respectively, are\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}=\\{3,6,9,12\\},\\,{S}_{Y}=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4402cb5-3b8b-556e-9fb0-20470cdb8422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519599 #edb/ref 1074 "Note the following:\n\n- For a given joint PMF $f(x,y),$ we can think of each point where $f(x,y)\\ne 0$ as a point mass in the plane, where the \"probability mass\" at each point equals the probability associated with that point.\n\n- The joint CDF $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the total \"probability mass\" that lies in the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axis.\n\nGiven the supports of $X$ and $Y,$ the joint support $S$ is a subset of\n\n$$\n{S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}=\\{3,6,9,12\\}\\times \\{2,4,6,8\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's plot the points in ${S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y},$ which contains the joint support, and sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a7/a782578ef55726c98ed23d89cf3de531ede60fe5db3fa9062c05507c5c2f60af.png)\n\nNotice that this region encompasses the same possible support points as the infinite rectangle with its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ and top-left corner at $(3,4),$ where the top and right boundaries are included, and the left boundary is excluded.\n\n![](images/7b/7ba99957a743b5cf283c2668e81b6c1aeb06730d47fc157aec90fe8bc880ced4.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(12,4)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* all points in the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(3,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/3b/3bd652b8d918e26cd330ad43555f039845d0b5405b70c96828bb2d652b7f872c.png)\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 6,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(12,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 12,Y\\le 4)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(3,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 3,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(12,4)-F(3,4) \\\\  & =0.6-0.15 \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
